      "text": "When rolling 5 fair 12-sided dice, the probability of each die rolling a number from 1-12 is $\\frac{12}{12}$ or 1, or 100%. To roll even numbers, we need to roll an even number less than 13 on each die. Three of these values are odd: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, and 11. Since we are interested in the probability of rolling an even number on a fair 12-sided die, we can find the probability of rolling an even number as $\\frac{6}{12}$ (which is 0.5). Therefore, each die has a probability of 0.5 of rolling an even number. One roll of the die has a probability of 0.5 is $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^5$. When rolling 5 fair 12-sided dice, the total probability of rolling five dice with an even number when flipping all the dice is $(0.5)^5$. We can determine the total number of dice in the event that at least two of them will roll an even number. Thus, for $x$ even rolls, total probability will be given by:\n\\[\\boxed{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^5 \\times 12! \\times \\frac{\\left(\\binom{12}{2}\\right) \\times \\left(\\binom{10}{\\text{MAT}\\Delta\\text{TIMO}}\\right)}{\\binom{12}{5}}}.\\]",
